WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:14.249 --> 00:00:21.020
So today's session is on the globalization,
secularization and transformation of faiths

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:22.890
and

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:27.210
we don't have Tony Blair with us,

00:00:27.210 --> 00:00:32.239
we don't have of an additional resource
person with us, so you're kind of stuck with

00:00:32.239 --> 00:00:34.520
me and with Doug

00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:41.520
for this course. What I'll try
to do is maybe offer just a bit more

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:46.430
extended introductory comments on the topic
and then

00:00:46.430 --> 00:00:50.650
open it up for for discussion, and this may
be an opportunity also

00:00:50.650 --> 00:00:56.590
to kind of range a bit more broadly in terms of
of your questions about faith

00:00:56.590 --> 00:00:58.660
than just the topic of this

00:00:58.660 --> 00:01:03.860
session that's to look back

00:01:03.860 --> 00:01:07.000
as well as maybe anticipate the future.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:09.790
And then I thought what I might do

00:01:09.790 --> 00:01:10.650
in the second

00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:13.240
part of our

00:01:13.240 --> 00:01:16.010
session

00:01:16.010 --> 00:01:19.400
is to take the question that has surfaced

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:24.630
very prominently and very quickly in the session

00:01:24.630 --> 00:01:27.430
which we had with Tony Blair,

00:01:27.430 --> 00:01:30.510
and that is the question of religious exclusivism

00:01:30.510 --> 00:01:34.380
and the relationship between religious exclusivism

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:35.440
and

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:40.110
a kind of promotion of

00:01:40.110 --> 00:01:41.960
pluralism as a

00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:45.630
social value or as a political project.

00:01:45.630 --> 00:01:48.870
The relationship between exclusive religious
claims

00:01:48.870 --> 00:01:54.220
and a pluralistic environments in which we
find ourselves and in which globalization processes

00:01:54.220 --> 00:01:56.280
are increasingly created. 

00:01:56.280 --> 00:02:01.640
So I have developed some comments on
this. It was an essential

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:04.220
element, almost a red thread of the

00:02:04.220 --> 00:02:09.429
the discussions last year.
I think it was the first session that it was raised

00:02:09.429 --> 00:02:10.779
just like you did,

00:02:10.779 --> 00:02:14.989
which we should have built in

00:02:14.989 --> 00:02:21.099
reading about it and time
for reflection on this. We didn't but as

00:02:21.099 --> 00:02:23.049
it turns out we'll have probably

00:02:23.049 --> 00:02:27.449
thirty five, forty minutes on that.

00:02:27.449 --> 00:02:29.600
That's as long as you want to deal

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:31.829
with the issue.

00:02:31.829 --> 00:02:33.559
So, first

00:02:33.559 --> 00:02:37.609
we talked a bit about significance
of faith. We don't need to repeat any

00:02:37.609 --> 00:02:42.899
of this and we are obviously dealing with the question of secularization

00:02:42.899 --> 00:02:44.519
and transformation of faiths.

00:02:44.519 --> 00:02:46.689
We're obviously dealing with the future

00:02:46.689 --> 00:02:50.409
of faiths in the globalized world

00:02:50.409 --> 00:02:51.209
and

00:02:51.209 --> 00:02:53.319
that future concerns

00:02:53.319 --> 00:02:56.439
questions of religious adherence

00:02:56.439 --> 00:03:01.239
will faiths, do faiths continue to claim the hearts
and minds of people around the globe

00:03:01.239 --> 00:03:02.879
as much as they did

00:03:02.879 --> 00:03:05.380
in the past? For instance that's a

00:03:05.380 --> 00:03:08.150
question we need to answer and

00:03:08.150 --> 00:03:13.089
the answer is yes, in particular Islam and
Christianity, Buddhism,

00:03:13.089 --> 00:03:17.829
these three faiths are growing in numerical terms, in relative and absolute terms as

00:03:17.829 --> 00:03:19.489
I said

00:03:19.489 --> 00:03:20.129
previously.

00:03:20.129 --> 00:03:23.019
Will they do so in the future? Well,

00:03:23.019 --> 00:03:26.090
they'll most likely

00:03:26.090 --> 00:03:29.419
if you extrapolate from the basis of data that we have now,

00:03:29.419 --> 00:03:32.339
it's likely it's going to continue

00:03:32.339 --> 00:03:33.719
to be that way.

00:03:33.719 --> 00:03:37.389
What will those numbers be? Well, it's tough
to tough to say,

00:03:37.389 --> 00:03:39.130
but it does look

00:03:39.130 --> 00:03:43.200
like in the immediate future at least the
world's going to get to be

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:45.239
a bit more--

00:03:45.239 --> 00:03:47.819
for some I should say an even more--

00:03:47.819 --> 00:03:51.829
religious place than it is right now.

00:03:51.829 --> 00:03:56.989
Now the connected question to
this is not just that of religious

00:03:56.989 --> 00:03:59.720
adherence;

00:03:59.720 --> 00:04:04.870
we're also interested in the role of faith in
public realm. Are faiths

00:04:04.870 --> 00:04:07.479
a force that shapes realities

00:04:07.479 --> 00:04:09.559
beyond people's private

00:04:09.559 --> 00:04:11.749
and narrowly communal lives?

00:04:11.749 --> 00:04:14.569
And we've seen previously arguments which would
suggest

00:04:14.569 --> 00:04:20.650
no, faiths are something very much private.
Faiths are if you want belief in the phenomenal.

00:04:20.650 --> 00:04:24.479
They don't shape anything, they are shaped by
something, conditioned by other things,

00:04:24.479 --> 00:04:26.180
but they themselves are

00:04:26.180 --> 00:04:29.650
some people would even argue sternly causally inert.

00:04:29.650 --> 00:04:32.639
They don't do anything, right? They just

00:04:34.280 --> 00:04:37.149
come to surface given certain conditions.

00:04:37.149 --> 00:04:41.159
I'm pretty sure 
that's not quite right but at least that's

00:04:41.159 --> 00:04:42.969
one of the possibilities.

00:04:42.969 --> 00:04:46.219
And they are shaping

00:04:46.219 --> 00:04:49.560
realities beyond private and narrow communal
lives of people.

00:04:49.560 --> 00:04:51.880
Will they continue to do so in the future,

00:04:51.880 --> 00:04:55.210
and in what ways faiths will be significant?

00:04:55.210 --> 00:04:58.369
And then there's a question also of the character

00:04:58.369 --> 00:04:59.700
of faiths.

00:04:59.700 --> 00:05:01.509
How is the

00:05:01.509 --> 00:05:04.740
character, the forms that faiths take

00:05:04.740 --> 00:05:06.340
being shaped

00:05:06.340 --> 00:05:08.150
by globalization

00:05:08.150 --> 00:05:09.999
processes,

00:05:09.999 --> 00:05:13.050
and

00:05:13.050 --> 00:05:13.859
given

00:05:13.859 --> 00:05:18.449
that faiths are being shaped by globalization processes if they indeed are,

00:05:18.449 --> 00:05:21.099
how do the influences go

00:05:21.099 --> 00:05:23.829
in complex ways?

00:05:23.829 --> 00:05:25.380
So,

00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:29.550
that's roughly what we're going to be talking
about, but first a few comments on secularization.

00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:33.599
So, I want to give you

00:05:33.599 --> 00:05:36.199
a few,

00:05:36.199 --> 00:05:41.870
mention a few aspects of secularization.
Often when we talk but secularization

00:05:41.870 --> 00:05:46.120
we end up talking past
each other because

00:05:46.120 --> 00:05:48.240
the word secularization is used

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:54.500
in at least three distinct ways, and
it's important to know what we're

00:05:54.500 --> 00:05:57.389
dealing, how we're using the word

00:05:57.389 --> 00:05:59.719
when we're using it. So,

00:05:59.719 --> 00:06:02.990
first sense of secularization is

00:06:02.990 --> 00:06:05.749
differentiation

00:06:05.749 --> 00:06:09.479
of secular spheres,

00:06:09.479 --> 00:06:14.729
which is to say the differentiation of society
and in that society

00:06:14.729 --> 00:06:16.990
emancipation of, say, state

00:06:16.990 --> 00:06:18.319
of economy

00:06:18.319 --> 00:06:20.769
of media, broad

00:06:20.769 --> 00:06:22.179
spheres of life,

00:06:22.179 --> 00:06:25.800
from immediate or from even media influence

00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:28.449
of religious institutions

00:06:28.449 --> 00:06:33.219
and possibly even religious norms.

00:06:33.219 --> 00:06:34.740
Now, adverse of

00:06:34.740 --> 00:06:35.479
this

00:06:35.479 --> 00:06:37.880
is what sociologists call

00:06:37.880 --> 00:06:39.090
privatization of religion,

00:06:39.090 --> 00:06:44.539
that is if religion is not shaping how state
is being run, how economy is being run, well,

00:06:44.539 --> 00:06:47.089
then religion is

00:06:47.089 --> 00:06:49.139
increasingly has become a private affair

00:06:49.139 --> 00:06:50.490
of individuals.

00:06:50.490 --> 00:06:54.370
So that secularization in one sense
is differentiation.

00:06:54.370 --> 00:06:58.830
Second sense in which secularization is used

00:06:58.830 --> 00:07:00.589
is simply decline

00:07:00.589 --> 00:07:07.589
of religious belief in practice.

00:07:08.449 --> 00:07:12.359
People simply turning away from belief in God,

00:07:12.359 --> 00:07:18.019
and people turning away from religious practices that
there may be discrepancies there, right?

00:07:18.019 --> 00:07:20.789
Many people can say that they believe in God,

00:07:20.789 --> 00:07:22.699
but they don't

00:07:22.699 --> 00:07:24.669
attend worship services,

00:07:24.669 --> 00:07:28.580
or they worship services that don't
very much matter in their lives.

00:07:28.580 --> 00:07:30.210
There's ways in which

00:07:30.210 --> 00:07:32.379
this decline of religious belief

00:07:32.379 --> 00:07:34.710
and practice can be

00:07:34.710 --> 00:07:35.809
expressed.

00:07:35.809 --> 00:07:37.840
And then final

00:07:37.840 --> 00:07:38.759
sense

00:07:38.759 --> 00:07:41.409
is change in

00:07:41.409 --> 00:07:45.489
conditions of belief,

00:07:45.489 --> 00:07:47.179
and this has been introduced

00:07:47.179 --> 00:07:48.769
in a book,

00:07:48.769 --> 00:07:53.199
most compelling in a book

00:07:53.199 --> 00:07:55.210
by Charles Taylor,

00:07:55.210 --> 00:07:57.449
"A Secular Age".

00:07:57.449 --> 00:07:59.319
It's a kind of doorstopper

00:07:59.319 --> 00:08:03.629
book. It can inflict serious damage

00:08:03.629 --> 00:08:05.649
if you wield it

00:08:05.649 --> 00:08:07.900
inappropriate or in in inappropriate ways.

00:08:09.229 --> 00:08:13.770
And it's a long, windy story which
Charles Taylor tells,

00:08:13.770 --> 00:08:16.889
but he is one of the the great philosophers
of out time,

00:08:16.889 --> 00:08:17.950
and this is really one of

00:08:17.950 --> 00:08:20.789
great books on secularization

00:08:20.789 --> 00:08:24.289
issue, but his question is

00:08:24.289 --> 00:08:26.649
how did we come

00:08:26.649 --> 00:08:30.919
to move away from a society where belief in God

00:08:30.919 --> 00:08:34.010
was unchallenged and unproblematic,

00:08:34.010 --> 00:08:35.950
something like fifteen

00:08:35.950 --> 00:08:38.250
hundreds, right,

00:08:38.250 --> 00:08:44.450
to a society or to a cultural environment in
which faith is understood to be one option

00:08:44.450 --> 00:08:46.370
among others

00:08:46.370 --> 00:08:48.420
and often an option which is not

00:08:48.420 --> 00:08:51.540
the easiest one to embrace.

00:08:51.540 --> 00:08:54.770
So he is asking

00:08:54.770 --> 00:09:00.250
conditions of belief have shifted in such a way

00:09:00.250 --> 00:09:01.520
that

00:09:01.520 --> 00:09:03.340
you have to choose

00:09:03.340 --> 00:09:07.030
that faith among multiplicity of options rather
than kind of

00:09:07.030 --> 00:09:08.450
slipping into it

00:09:08.450 --> 00:09:11.310
as a state of normalcy in which society

00:09:11.310 --> 00:09:13.710
operates.

00:09:13.710 --> 00:09:15.290
Now we will be

00:09:15.290 --> 00:09:20.060
interested primarily in the first two aspects of secularization,

00:09:20.060 --> 00:09:24.290
in decline of individual
religious practice,

00:09:24.290 --> 00:09:28.180
and belief can be seen as an aspect of practice,

00:09:28.180 --> 00:09:29.949
and secondly in decline

00:09:29.949 --> 00:09:36.730
of cultural influence of faiths.

00:09:36.730 --> 00:09:39.100
I don't think we should bother too much

00:09:39.100 --> 00:09:40.900
with a question about

00:09:40.900 --> 00:09:41.930
the future

00:09:41.930 --> 00:09:43.210
of religion,

00:09:43.210 --> 00:09:45.529
and often secularization is

00:09:45.529 --> 00:09:46.960
discussed in those

00:09:46.960 --> 00:09:48.400
terms.

00:09:50.060 --> 00:09:52.450
And some people then expect

00:09:52.450 --> 00:09:55.290
eventual demise of

00:09:55.290 --> 00:09:56.070
religion

00:09:57.179 --> 00:09:57.910
almost like a

00:09:57.910 --> 00:09:58.829
as a matter of

00:09:58.829 --> 00:10:02.680
linear understanding of history. It's part
of the philosophy of history that religion

00:10:02.680 --> 00:10:03.520
ought to

00:10:03.520 --> 00:10:05.160
or that religion will

00:10:05.160 --> 00:10:06.599
experience demise.

00:10:06.599 --> 00:10:09.090
Or as kind of projectoral

00:10:09.090 --> 00:10:12.290
extrapolation from dots, and we're not

00:10:12.290 --> 00:10:16.149
so much interested in that, not just because
this is not easy to answer this question,

00:10:16.149 --> 00:10:18.310
but because I think for the purposes of

00:10:18.310 --> 00:10:19.850
this course it's not

00:10:19.850 --> 00:10:20.619
necessary for us

00:10:20.619 --> 00:10:22.380
to answer this question. We're

00:10:22.380 --> 00:10:23.730
talking about next decades;

00:10:23.730 --> 00:10:30.730
we're not talking about ultimate future

00:10:32.830 --> 00:10:33.880
of religion.

00:10:33.880 --> 00:10:36.500
So, let's now look at

00:10:36.500 --> 00:10:40.560
the relationship between globalization and
secularization.

00:10:40.560 --> 00:10:42.120
now one thing I think

00:10:42.120 --> 00:10:45.030
is right to say

00:10:45.030 --> 00:10:48.540
is that globalization

00:10:48.540 --> 00:10:49.480
is

00:10:49.480 --> 00:10:54.940
and entails modernization.

00:10:54.940 --> 00:11:01.410
Globalization is fueled, maybe primarily driven
by, modern market system.

00:11:01.410 --> 00:11:04.890
It's mediated and helped by

00:11:04.890 --> 00:11:06.550
technological advancements,

00:11:06.550 --> 00:11:12.310
especially advancements in communication, but
other also technological advances.

00:11:12.310 --> 00:11:13.010
And

00:11:13.010 --> 00:11:16.100
I think globalization is

00:11:16.100 --> 00:11:18.820
assuming and it's also

00:11:18.820 --> 00:11:20.570
reinforcing

00:11:20.570 --> 00:11:24.730
what sociologists call individuation,

00:11:24.730 --> 00:11:27.220
so to say that individual is

00:11:27.220 --> 00:11:32.890
you've seen that I think that Berger mentions that individual is taken out of

00:11:34.890 --> 00:11:36.830
sort of traditional,

00:11:36.830 --> 00:11:38.940
given identities

00:11:38.940 --> 00:11:41.529
and thrown on his or her

00:11:41.529 --> 00:11:43.590
own resources to make choices

00:11:43.590 --> 00:11:47.630
about life.

00:11:47.630 --> 00:11:49.650
And globalization

00:11:49.650 --> 00:11:51.290
also means

00:11:51.290 --> 00:11:56.040
rather fast-paced fashioning and refashioning

00:11:56.040 --> 00:11:57.770
of

00:11:57.770 --> 00:12:00.230
alternative, almost global culture.

00:12:00.230 --> 00:12:04.739
I think something has happened on the global level
that's moving very fast in terms of cultural

00:12:04.739 --> 00:12:07.140
change.

00:12:07.140 --> 00:12:08.649
So all of these

00:12:08.649 --> 00:12:11.090
elements I think are elements of modernization,

00:12:11.090 --> 00:12:14.790
but now the question is whether

00:12:14.790 --> 00:12:16.430
modernization

00:12:16.430 --> 00:12:17.720
entails

00:12:17.720 --> 00:12:19.080
secularization.

00:12:19.080 --> 00:12:24.480
In authority one is the same modernization,
to modernize is to secularize,

00:12:24.480 --> 00:12:26.800
and modernization

00:12:26.800 --> 00:12:28.470
entails secularization,

00:12:28.470 --> 00:12:31.120
decline of religion in society,

00:12:31.120 --> 00:12:35.010
decline of religion in the minds of individuals,
then obviously

00:12:35.010 --> 00:12:36.270
globalization, too,

00:12:36.270 --> 00:12:37.160
will

00:12:37.160 --> 00:12:39.960
entail secularization.

00:12:39.960 --> 00:12:43.790
If religion survives then,

00:12:43.790 --> 00:12:49.460
they survive because they're holding something from the past.

00:12:49.460 --> 00:12:54.080
You know because modernization hasn't progressed
far enough

00:12:54.080 --> 00:12:55.050
yet, or

00:12:55.050 --> 00:12:56.160
because of social

00:12:56.160 --> 00:12:57.260
inertia

00:12:57.260 --> 00:12:58.970
or resurgence of religion

00:12:58.970 --> 00:13:01.760
can be kind of last gasp of religion

00:13:01.760 --> 00:13:02.940
before its final

00:13:02.940 --> 00:13:04.670
demise.

00:13:06.360 --> 00:13:10.290
Law professor here for whom I have great admiration,
it was my friend,

00:13:10.290 --> 00:13:13.730
Tony Kronman, in conversations with
him he basically

00:13:13.730 --> 00:13:17.380
expressed this opinion; what you see
this religious resurgence

00:13:17.380 --> 00:13:20.700
is the kind of last gasp of religion before hah,

00:13:20.700 --> 00:13:23.370
it gives out its

00:13:23.370 --> 00:13:26.470
ghost.

00:13:26.470 --> 00:13:28.240
The other alternative is

00:13:28.240 --> 00:13:29.489
modernization

00:13:29.489 --> 00:13:34.540
is not necessarily secularization,

00:13:34.540 --> 00:13:36.690
which means

00:13:36.690 --> 00:13:38.460
that there might be

00:13:38.460 --> 00:13:39.920
religious ways

00:13:39.920 --> 00:13:43.420
to be modern.

00:13:43.420 --> 00:13:45.620
Embracing secularism

00:13:45.620 --> 00:13:50.520
is one way to modernize.

00:13:50.520 --> 00:13:54.030
Embracing faith

00:13:54.030 --> 00:13:56.620
and holding onto faith

00:13:56.620 --> 00:14:00.950
maybe an alternative way to modernize.

00:14:00.950 --> 00:14:03.630
We'll encounter this concept

00:14:03.630 --> 00:14:06.420
of alternative modernities

00:14:06.420 --> 00:14:09.560
in the next session, especially the work of

00:14:09.560 --> 00:14:12.590
Jose Casanova, who will be our guest here,

00:14:12.590 --> 00:14:15.230
and I would encourage you to read carefully

00:14:15.230 --> 00:14:16.870
the text assigned

00:14:16.870 --> 00:14:20.750
for the next week, his text assigned for the next
week. I think that's one of the best

00:14:20.750 --> 00:14:23.660
sociological analysis of what's happening

00:14:23.660 --> 00:14:24.840
in the world of religion

00:14:24.840 --> 00:14:26.260
today.

00:14:26.260 --> 00:14:29.790
Charles Taylor himself is a strong and
sturdy

00:14:29.790 --> 00:14:32.420
mainly philosophical advocate

00:14:32.420 --> 00:14:38.470
but also sociological advocate of
alternative modernities.

00:14:38.470 --> 00:14:43.470
So, if there are such alternative modernities, religious ways to modernize,

00:14:43.470 --> 00:14:45.129
then obviously

00:14:45.129 --> 00:14:47.960
globalization need not lead to secularization,

00:14:47.960 --> 00:14:53.400
but as I sometimes say globalization
can be played in

00:14:53.400 --> 00:14:56.080
multiple different registers.

00:14:56.080 --> 00:15:00.540
There are different religious registers in which
globalization can be played,

00:15:00.540 --> 00:15:02.600
and there's also secular,

00:15:02.600 --> 00:15:05.200
presumably also multiple secular registers

00:15:05.200 --> 00:15:09.460
in which it can be played depending
on the particular culture

00:15:09.460 --> 00:15:10.650
in which

00:15:14.700 --> 00:15:15.880
through which

00:15:15.880 --> 00:15:22.880
globalization processes refract themselves.

00:15:23.130 --> 00:15:26.670
So,

00:15:29.750 --> 00:15:31.720
but the question for us is

00:15:31.720 --> 00:15:35.380
not simply whether

00:15:35.380 --> 00:15:39.270
it's possible for religion to

00:15:39.270 --> 00:15:41.190
live together

00:15:41.190 --> 00:15:47.070
with advancing globalization processes, and my argument
would be that secularization thesis has proven

00:15:47.070 --> 00:15:49.760
not to be correct

00:15:49.760 --> 00:15:51.390
at least for now,

00:15:51.390 --> 00:15:52.359
but also

00:15:52.359 --> 00:15:55.710
what might be the impact of globalization
processes

00:15:55.710 --> 00:15:56.990
on the shape

00:15:56.990 --> 00:15:58.390
which religions take.

00:15:58.390 --> 00:16:05.390
And so I want to talk about now about globalization's
impact on faith.

00:16:08.200 --> 00:16:13.290
There are a few points that I want to make here,

00:16:13.290 --> 00:16:15.110
one of them being

00:16:15.110 --> 00:16:20.740
globalization and the conditions under which
people embrace faith,

00:16:20.740 --> 00:16:25.580
globalization and the form which faiths take,

00:16:25.580 --> 00:16:29.670
globalization and the modes of transmission
of faith

00:16:29.670 --> 00:16:33.220
and globalization and the modes of religious
influence,

00:16:33.220 --> 00:16:37.130
shaping the way that religion influences.

00:16:37.130 --> 00:16:39.200
So overarching conditions

00:16:39.200 --> 00:16:45.400
under which people embrace faith,

00:16:45.400 --> 00:16:51.150
but often it boils down to something like
a malcontent with globalization processes

00:16:51.150 --> 00:16:57.110
or with life in general, right? Religious
people,

00:16:57.110 --> 00:16:58.900
actually that would be too...

00:16:58.900 --> 00:17:02.190
I can give a whole lecture

00:17:02.190 --> 00:17:04.740
about the positive experience of

00:17:04.740 --> 00:17:05.430
life

00:17:05.430 --> 00:17:09.269
in association with embrace of religion,
but we'll

00:17:09.269 --> 00:17:10.570
keep that later for another

00:17:10.570 --> 00:17:11.920
class,

00:17:11.920 --> 00:17:13.360
another course.

00:17:13.360 --> 00:17:15.830
So, uh 

00:17:15.830 --> 00:17:19.450
for instance people experience
themselves as left out

00:17:19.450 --> 00:17:21.819
from globalization's benefits, 

00:17:21.819 --> 00:17:26.200
and they're seeking consolation in faith

00:17:26.200 --> 00:17:29.350
or possibly seeking inspiration to change

00:17:29.350 --> 00:17:33.040
the rules so as to attend

00:17:33.040 --> 00:17:38.240
to the losses of the so-called losers.

00:17:38.240 --> 00:17:41.900
A variation of that is the experience of injustice

00:17:41.900 --> 00:17:45.160
as a consequence of globalization

00:17:45.160 --> 00:17:48.780
and maybe finding in faith

00:17:48.780 --> 00:17:49.800
assurance 

00:17:49.800 --> 00:17:52.370
of future justice

00:17:52.370 --> 00:17:56.700
or seeking motivation in faith to
struggle for justice.

00:17:56.700 --> 00:17:59.050
You know that did this idea of

00:17:59.050 --> 00:18:02.310
finding assurance in faith of future justice

00:18:02.310 --> 00:18:07.260
is a very interesting how it emerged immediately after

00:18:07.260 --> 00:18:08.659
World War 2,

00:18:08.659 --> 00:18:12.280
and among some very prominent Jewish
thinkers.

00:18:12.280 --> 00:18:15.559
And the basic idea, the basic intuition
was

00:18:17.660 --> 00:18:20.190
it's somehow unacceptable

00:18:20.190 --> 00:18:21.890
that the torturer

00:18:21.890 --> 00:18:23.970
would eternally triumph

00:18:23.970 --> 00:18:26.400
over the victim.

00:18:26.400 --> 00:18:28.710
Some kind of sense of

00:18:28.710 --> 00:18:31.159
that immense defeat

00:18:31.159 --> 00:18:33.440
that Holocaust was

00:18:33.440 --> 00:18:36.090
and the immense wrong that it

00:18:36.090 --> 00:18:41.640
was and somehow crying for justice, a
search for Justice,

00:18:43.330 --> 00:18:45.380
It's a very interesting small book

00:18:49.370 --> 00:18:51.610
The longing

00:18:51.610 --> 00:18:52.650
for

00:18:52.650 --> 00:18:54.650
a radically different one.

00:18:54.650 --> 00:18:55.950
Is that a good translation?

00:19:00.310 --> 00:19:01.770
Third,

00:19:01.770 --> 00:19:06.600
reason for embrace faith, believeing the
globalization undermines local cultures

00:19:06.600 --> 00:19:13.260
and seeking affirmation of local cultures
through faith.

00:19:13.260 --> 00:19:18.200
We see that very easily in former Yugoslavia,
right? It's partly connected with globalization process.

00:19:18.200 --> 00:19:22.440
How faith attached itself to 
itself to particular

00:19:22.440 --> 00:19:24.030
ethnic identities.

00:19:24.030 --> 00:19:28.370
Or experiencing disorientation in globalization

00:19:28.370 --> 00:19:31.830
kind of being caught in the midst of globalization
can feel like

00:19:31.830 --> 00:19:34.720
being in the swirling storm,

00:19:34.720 --> 00:19:40.890
and then seeking guidance and certainty in faith.

00:19:40.890 --> 00:19:47.400
Or sensing the meaninglessness of the ends
prevalent within globalization process,

00:19:47.400 --> 00:19:50.179
and seeking meaning in faith.

00:19:50.179 --> 00:19:53.640
I'm not suggesting that all people do it but some
religious people

00:19:53.640 --> 00:19:58.850
that's how they experience globalization and then they seek meaning.

00:19:58.850 --> 00:20:04.210
Or believing that globalization driven by
market forces undermines human flourishing

00:20:04.210 --> 00:20:10.180
by over-privileging say consumers and
simply reinforcing rather than shaping human desires.

00:20:10.180 --> 00:20:11.250
Something of that sort.

00:20:11.250 --> 00:20:14.210
And then seeking alternative modes of human
flourishing

00:20:14.210 --> 00:20:16.790
in faiths.

00:20:16.790 --> 00:20:20.960
The globalization is shaping the very form
which faith takes

00:20:20.960 --> 00:20:23.419
It's interesting phenomenon is

00:20:23.419 --> 00:20:24.970
democratization of faith,

00:20:24.970 --> 00:20:26.700
its steady

00:20:26.700 --> 00:20:28.230
growth in the processes of

00:20:28.230 --> 00:20:29.869
internal democratization

00:20:29.869 --> 00:20:33.070
of faith's subversion of authoritarian forms
of faith

00:20:33.070 --> 00:20:37.010
and even the forms of faith that have a strong
authority structures

00:20:37.010 --> 00:20:38.259
nonetheless

00:20:38.259 --> 00:20:39.480
incorporate in its own

00:20:39.480 --> 00:20:42.789
internal structure democratic

00:20:42.789 --> 00:20:44.720
elements.

00:20:44.720 --> 00:20:48.529
Non-authoritarian governments and active
participation,

00:20:48.529 --> 00:20:50.850
especially interesting here is inclusion of
women

00:20:50.850 --> 00:20:57.450
in leadership roles
and empowerment of women in

00:20:57.450 --> 00:21:00.620
leadership roles.

00:21:00.620 --> 00:21:06.530
But also what's happening is that faiths tend
to be moored from particular locales

00:21:06.530 --> 00:21:07.020
and

00:21:07.020 --> 00:21:10.150
they tend to become global faiths, or put it
differently

00:21:10.150 --> 00:21:11.350
it's only

00:21:11.350 --> 00:21:15.650
easily transportable global faiths that seems
to be

00:21:15.650 --> 00:21:18.919
surviving best and best adopted to

00:21:18.919 --> 00:21:23.880
the world's caught
in globalization processes.

00:21:23.880 --> 00:21:25.900
Somewhat in tension with this

00:21:25.900 --> 00:21:27.260
is the idea

00:21:27.260 --> 00:21:30.419
of rediscovery of culturally specific
forms the Fed

00:21:30.419 --> 00:21:33.830
and you can see how globalization processes
might require,

00:21:34.990 --> 00:21:38.720
must reckon cultural identities and faiths made and

00:21:38.720 --> 00:21:44.640
take forms that are culturally specific.

00:21:44.640 --> 00:21:47.650
And then interesting phenomenon and we
touched

00:21:47.650 --> 00:21:48.869
briefly on this

00:21:48.869 --> 00:21:51.520
is smorgasbord religiosity,

00:21:51.520 --> 00:21:53.350
especially find it in western

00:21:53.350 --> 00:21:56.009
culture or in kind of higher echelons
of

00:21:56.009 --> 00:21:58.980
global culture.

00:21:58.980 --> 00:22:01.880
It's kind of individually created

00:22:01.880 --> 00:22:04.120
faith tailored to individual

00:22:04.120 --> 00:22:06.919
needs. You've got nuggets of wisdom from
here and there.

00:22:06.919 --> 00:22:11.020
You combine those, and you are then Jewbu, is that right,

00:22:11.020 --> 00:22:15.749
well, or whatever else combination you can attach.

00:22:15.749 --> 00:22:18.290
That's not exhaustive description, right,

00:22:18.290 --> 00:22:23.590
of that and there are other permutations
and combinations of faiths and they're

00:22:23.590 --> 00:22:25.619
tailor made to a particular person,

00:22:25.619 --> 00:22:27.700
and they change also over time.

00:22:27.700 --> 00:22:30.419
How your needs change, so your

00:22:30.419 --> 00:22:32.760
religious sensibilities, and what you find
useful

00:22:32.760 --> 00:22:36.200
from different religious traditions, so that
changes

00:22:36.200 --> 00:22:37.220
as well.

00:22:37.220 --> 00:22:38.829
And they're containing generally

00:22:38.829 --> 00:22:41.250
kind of no overarching interpretation of

00:22:41.250 --> 00:22:43.730
reality. They tend to be more

00:22:43.730 --> 00:22:49.940
kind of spiritualities which energize,
which calm and soothe

00:22:49.940 --> 00:22:51.690
rather than kind of order

00:22:51.690 --> 00:22:53.730
the world as traditional

00:22:53.730 --> 00:23:00.660
spiritual traditional faiths that...

00:23:00.660 --> 00:23:02.399
And they have often a relatively highly

00:23:02.399 --> 00:23:05.700
attenuated sense of ultimacy.

00:23:05.700 --> 00:23:07.510
It's not that you have some

00:23:07.510 --> 00:23:11.900
sturdy divinity up there, right, but rather
you're finding the God in the self,

00:23:11.900 --> 00:23:13.240
that somehow there is

00:23:13.240 --> 00:23:20.240
in each individual again divinities
tailor-made to the self.

00:23:23.480 --> 00:23:28.730
C, globalization is shaping modes of transmission
of faith.

00:23:28.730 --> 00:23:32.680
Just go back a little bit
about

00:23:32.680 --> 00:23:35.890
this smorgasbord religiosity.

00:23:35.890 --> 00:23:38.900
It also turns faiths into

00:23:38.900 --> 00:23:42.110
kind of a lifestyle choice

00:23:42.110 --> 00:23:45.470
that cannot be contested by others. In other words it
takes the faith

00:23:45.470 --> 00:23:48.590
out of the domain of truth claims,

00:23:48.590 --> 00:23:50.779
claims about what is true and claims

00:23:50.779 --> 00:23:52.600
about what is called traditional faiths.

00:23:52.600 --> 00:23:54.510
They generally understand

00:23:54.510 --> 00:23:57.070
themselves as containing claims

00:23:57.070 --> 00:23:59.780
about the world that are considered to be
true

00:23:59.780 --> 00:24:01.700
and moral claims also.

00:24:01.700 --> 00:24:05.350
A smorgasbord religiosity is a more
kind of a

00:24:05.350 --> 00:24:06.460
lifestyle

00:24:06.460 --> 00:24:07.210
religiosity.

00:24:07.210 --> 00:24:09.840
They are preferences in the ways in which we

00:24:09.840 --> 00:24:11.670
seek to conduct our lives

00:24:11.670 --> 00:24:12.600
with kind of

00:24:12.600 --> 00:24:14.440
a bit of religious aura

00:24:14.440 --> 00:24:15.220
to them.

00:24:15.220 --> 00:24:20.620
That's one way to put it.

00:24:20.620 --> 00:24:25.070
Globalization is shaping modes of transmission of faith,

00:24:25.070 --> 00:24:26.510
technologies of delivery.

00:24:26.510 --> 00:24:30.940
Faiths are as present on internet
as they were

00:24:30.940 --> 00:24:36.799
when radio was invented, as they were when
TV was in its heyday. TV evangelists,

00:24:36.799 --> 00:24:41.000
notorious as they were, were ubiquitous.

00:24:41.000 --> 00:24:45.670
I remember the first time I came to this
country, and I came as a student of theology, you know,

00:24:45.670 --> 00:24:47.360
a very religious person

00:24:47.360 --> 00:24:48.669
I was.

00:24:48.669 --> 00:24:52.480
I stayed with a family and flipped through
channels

00:24:54.400 --> 00:24:57.530
and came across this guys whose name was Ernest Angley,

00:24:57.530 --> 00:25:04.409
dressed in this flamboyant
dress with a toupee on his head,

00:25:04.409 --> 00:25:10.460
walking in this haute, airy way and then coming to people and 
taking ghosts, 

00:25:10.460 --> 00:25:12.840
evil spirits of the ears, and I look,

00:25:12.840 --> 00:25:19.840
my goodness where have I landed. 
Is this religiosity in America? Wow.

00:25:21.430 --> 00:25:24.090
Everything's possible, but in person you realize
that,

00:25:24.090 --> 00:25:28.380
you know, that America is the land
of extremes, right, and everything in between,

00:25:28.380 --> 00:25:31.650
also.

00:25:31.650 --> 00:25:35.980
So technologies of delivery, right? New technologies
of delivery,

00:25:35.980 --> 00:25:40.370
which I think feeds also into democratization
of religions

00:25:40.370 --> 00:25:42.990
with very little formal authority

00:25:42.990 --> 00:25:49.040
or authority that is earned in act, and so
the authority that is transmitted

00:25:49.040 --> 00:25:50.820
through mass media often is

00:25:50.820 --> 00:25:53.400
precisely such kind of authority, 
especially

00:25:53.400 --> 00:25:55.640
through internet, right?

00:25:55.640 --> 00:25:58.809
New missionary movements

00:25:58.809 --> 00:26:00.490
from east to west

00:26:00.490 --> 00:26:04.400
not just from west to east. We're used to kind of thinking
Christianity as a missionary

00:26:04.400 --> 00:26:07.130
religion goes from west to east.

00:26:07.130 --> 00:26:11.240
The missionary movements goes the other way around.
Buddhism, the spread of buddhism for instance is

00:26:11.240 --> 00:26:15.050
a very good example of that.

00:26:15.050 --> 00:26:16.980
Or, the mission goes

00:26:16.980 --> 00:26:18.590
from non-western to non-western

00:26:18.590 --> 00:26:22.120
country. There's more missionary activities of
Christians

00:26:22.120 --> 00:26:22.820
between

00:26:22.820 --> 00:26:29.520
non-western countries than between

00:26:29.520 --> 00:26:30.729
west and the western countries.

00:26:30.729 --> 00:26:34.999
And then, Peter Berger has emphasized that, and 
also kind of the response to Peter Berger

00:26:34.999 --> 00:26:38.410
in part by Hans Joas.

00:26:38.410 --> 00:26:40.890
Transmission of faith in the context

00:26:40.890 --> 00:26:44.800
of competitive religious pluralism.

00:26:44.800 --> 00:26:46.299
There's religious pluralism,

00:26:46.299 --> 00:26:47.659
there's competition,

00:26:47.659 --> 00:26:49.240
and that's how,

00:26:49.240 --> 00:26:51.799
those are the conditions for

00:26:51.799 --> 00:26:55.590
transmission.

00:26:55.590 --> 00:27:01.270
And then finally globalization is shaping modes
of religious influence,

00:27:01.270 --> 00:27:05.940
which is to say how globalization is shaping
the way in which faiths engage

00:27:05.940 --> 00:27:09.750
globalization processes themselves.

00:27:09.750 --> 00:27:12.840
One option is more traditional one:

00:27:12.840 --> 00:27:15.210
religious authority,

00:27:15.210 --> 00:27:16.809
representing an institution,

00:27:16.809 --> 00:27:18.920
acting globally.

00:27:18.920 --> 00:27:20.200
For instance when

00:27:20.200 --> 00:27:22.340
Pope Benedict the sixteenth

00:27:22.340 --> 00:27:26.240
speaks, that's how he acts, the Dalai lama

00:27:26.240 --> 00:27:27.250
speaks, that's

00:27:27.250 --> 00:27:31.150
probably how he acts as the religious authority.

00:27:31.150 --> 00:27:33.890
But the other option is increasingly

00:27:33.890 --> 00:27:36.290
prominent

00:27:36.290 --> 00:27:37.080
and significant:

00:27:37.080 --> 00:27:39.290
Informal religious authority

00:27:39.290 --> 00:27:42.970
in the network of believers.

00:27:42.970 --> 00:27:49.730
And it's kind of shaping subcultural
movement of global dimensions.

00:27:49.730 --> 00:27:50.970
Bin Laden, right,

00:27:50.970 --> 00:27:53.460
is a good example.

00:27:53.460 --> 00:28:00.060
Or take it to American scene, most influential
religious figure in America is arguably Rick Warren,

00:28:00.060 --> 00:28:01.040
right?

00:28:02.600 --> 00:28:04.940
But his authority is

00:28:04.940 --> 00:28:07.330
clearly the authority of his books.

00:28:07.330 --> 00:28:10.060
He has no formal authority at all

00:28:10.060 --> 00:28:12.960
except maybe in his own Saddleback Church,
and yet

00:28:12.960 --> 00:28:18.000
his authority stretches not just in this
country but around the globe.

00:28:18.000 --> 00:28:19.990
That, too, is made possible I think by

00:28:19.990 --> 00:28:24.920
modernization and globalization processes.

